{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Aaron and his sons shall wash, etc.</b> This washing is a matter of honor for those above, for all who approach the table of kings to minister and to touch the loaf of the king and the wine of his banquet wash their hands, because the hands are constantly engaged. And He added here the washing of the feet, because the priests minister barefoot, and there are people who have filth and ugliness in their feet. And by way of truth, it is because the head of the man and his extremities are the hands and the feet, for the hands are above all his body when he raises them, and the feet are below, and in the form of man they are a allusion to the ten sefirot, so that his entire body is between them. And as they said in Sefer Yetzirah (ch. 6): He cut a covenant between the ten fingers of his hands and the ten fingers of his feet, with the circumcision of the tongue and the circumcision of the membrum. Therefore the ministers of the Most High were commanded to wash the hands and the feet. And this washing is for sanctity — Onkelos rendered it thus (Onkelos on Exodus 30:19), and from this matter our Rabbis instituted netilat yadayim for prayer, that one should intend this, in the manner of the lifting of the hands. And the washing is the commandment, but the kiyor (basin) He commanded for its preparation and it is not a prerequisite (Zevachim 21b) and not a commandment, for on Yom HaKippurim the Kohen Gadol sanctifies his hands and his feet from a golden kiton (vessel) that they would make in his honor (Yoma 43b), but we learn from the kiyor that a vessel is required."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The citation of Sefer Yetzirah chapter 'פרק ו' follows the standard six-chapter division; the verse reference for Onkelos follows the printed editions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:19:16.392827773Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}